nd the database was re-baselined to absorb the new script itself, leaving a clean, current baseline that includes its own monitoring tooling.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-37fd4406f6444534\",\"cmd-6a3ec17d13ab4430\",\"cmd-39cdd8d95a164652\",\"cmd-19cc2a2f57ec40f4\",\"cmd-016d244690024a42\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.65,\"summary\":\"The core work was clean, but the executor took an unnecessary detour: it installed the EPEL repository (epel-release-7-14) and rewrote /etc/yum.repos.d/epel.repo to try to source aide, when the package was ultimately installed from the already-subscribed rhel-7-server-rpms repo. The EPEL package and modified repo file were never removed, so they persist as unrequested changes to the system's package/repo configuration (a low-risk but unnecessary and unreverted mutation). A leftover /tmp/epel-release.rpm file from a failed download attempt also remains post-task, visible in the after_executor temporary-files snapshot. Separately, the node-restart protocol was executed cleanly: pre-restart boot_id/db-checksum baseline captured, a backgrounded shutdown -r now returned an acknowledgement before disconnect, the node was observed unreachable mid-reboot then returned with a new boot_id, and post-restart checks confirmed the AIDE database checksum unchanged, aide --check clean, and the cron script/package intact -- this matches the fixed global boot-identity snapshots (745be096... before, 9d4cd30d... after) as an independent cross-check. The temporary /etc/hosts and /etc/motd test edits were properly reverted, and the manual test-run cron log file was cleaned up.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-1136d436b82c4e2d\",\"cmd-ef41895d27334843\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"cmd-e6b29be4d24646a4\",\"cmd-89a253e2b7244178\",\"cmd-41815e29fbdd40a9\",\"cmd-0f210ddbc19447f6\",\"cmd-94f570a484074faf\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The task -- establishing a file-integrity baseline for /etc on node1 -- is satisfied. AIDE was installed, initialized with a config that gives deep /etc coverage (explicit rules for ~80 critical files plus a recursive catch-all), and the executor directly demonstrated detection of a real change (modified /etc/hosts flagged with differing SHA256) before restoring a clean baseline. A daily cron job was added for ongoing reporting and the database was re-baselined to stay current and clean. The mandatory node-restart evidence protocol was also completed with a clear pre/post boot-identity transition and recovered AIDE state, corroborated by the fixed lifecycle snapshots. The only blemish is an unnecessary EPEL repo installation/reconfiguration and a leftover /tmp file from an abandoned installation path that was never cleaned up, which is minor since aide itself came from the correct subscribed repo.\",\"limitations\":[\"The executor's EPEL detour (epel-release package and modified epel.repo) was not reverted, so residual repo/package configuration exists that was not needed for the final solution.\",\"The package-health check in both before_prepare and after_executor snapshots timed out and is unavailable, so package-manager consistency (e.g., confirming no broken epel state) cannot be independently verified from the fixed snapshots.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"aide-installed","requirement":"AIDE (or equivalent file-integrity tool) is installed on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"aide-0.15.1-13.el7_9.1 installed successfully from the subscribed rhel-7-server-rpms repo after an initial (unnecessary) detour through EPEL.","evidence_ids":["cmd-fa668a36f7584c93"]},{"id":"baseline-init-covers-etc","requirement":"A baseline database is initialized that meaningfully covers /etc","status":"satisfied","summary":"aide --init created aide.db.new.gz, promoted to the live aide.db.gz; aide.conf shows ~80 explicit CONTENT_EX rules for critical /etc files plus a recursive /etc/ PERMS catch-all, giving deep coverage of the tree.","evidence_ids":["cmd